<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What "coverage" is allowed to mean in this repository.

  Without this file coverlet instruments every assembly in the test output directory - 36 of
  them, EF Core and Npgsql and the whole framework included - and reports 5.1%. That number is
  not wrong so much as meaningless: it is measuring how much of Microsoft's code this project's
  tests happen to execute. Any figure quoted anywhere must come from this configuration, or it
  is not comparable to itself between runs.

  Included: the three production assemblies, and only those.
  The storefront is deliberately absent. It is a Blazor WebAssembly client whose behaviour is
  browser behaviour; these suites never load it, so including it would report a real 0% for a
  real assembly and drag a number that means something into meaning nothing. When browser tests
  exist, they can measure it - and until they do, the honest statement is that it is unmeasured
  rather than uncovered.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura</Format>

          <Include>[VelaCommerce.Domain]*,[VelaCommerce.Infrastructure]*,[VelaCommerce.Api]*</Include>

          <!--
            EF Core migrations are generated: a migration's Up/Down is asserted by the
            model-matches-migrations gate in CI and by every Testcontainers test that runs
            against the schema, neither of which shows up as line coverage. Counting them
            would let a large generated file move the number without anyone writing a test.
          -->
          <!--
            The second filter is about the TOOL, not about this codebase, and it has to be
            explained or it looks like someone hiding async code from the metric.

            coverlet 6.0.4 folds an async method's state machine back into the method that
            declares it: `<ListProductsAsync>d__7` is reported as part of CatalogEndpoints,
            which is what a reader expects, because the user's code is what it contains.
            coverlet 10.0.1 emits the state machine AS WELL, as its own class, while still
            folding it in - so every async body is counted twice, once as itself and once as
            compiler plumbing. This project is async end to end, so the effect is not small:
            coverable lines go 4,432 -> 8,472 and the reported figure falls 68.2% -> 61.6%
            without one line of code or one test changing. That is what failed the coverage
            floor on Dependabot's coverlet 6 -> 10 bump.

            ExcludeByAttribute does NOT close it. CompilerGeneratedAttribute was already in
            the list below and made no difference, fully qualified or not - measured, not
            assumed. Filtering the state-machine types by name does, and exactly: with it,
            coverlet 10 reports the same classes, files, covered lines and coverable lines as
            coverlet 6, to the line.

            It is a no-op on 6.0.4 - verified by running the full suite with and without it on
            the same commit and getting identical numbers - so it is safe to carry across the
            upgrade rather than needing to land with it.

            Lowering the floor to accommodate 61.6% was the other option and it was the wrong
            one: the tests cover exactly what they covered before, and a floor that moves to
            meet a measurement change is not a floor.
          -->
          <Exclude>[VelaCommerce.Infrastructure]VelaCommerce.Infrastructure.Persistence.Migrations.*,[VelaCommerce.*]*d__*</Exclude>

          <ExcludeByAttribute>Obsolete,GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>
          <SingleHit>false</SingleHit>
          <UseSourceLink>true</UseSourceLink>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
